      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "BACKGROUND",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 10,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The major causes of morbidity and mortality \namong children living with HIV in low- and \nmiddle-income countries are pneumonia \n(including Pneumocystis pneumonia), \ntuberculosis (TB), bloodstream infections, \ndiarrhoeal disease and severe acute malnutrition \n(1,2). In addition, for adolescents living with \nHIV, adult-type opportunistic infections also \noccur, including cryptococcal meningitis (3,4). \nWHO published guidelines on the management \nof advanced HIV disease in 2017, promoting a \npackage of interventions to prevent, identify \nand treat HIV-associated infections among \npeople with adva",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out the main causes of illness and death in children with HIV in low- and middle-income countries, such as pneumonia, TB and malnutrition, and explains that the brief builds on WHO's 2017 advanced HIV disease guidelines. It notes that many interventions also apply to children without advanced disease.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Current WHO guidelines on managing advanced HIV disease",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-current-who-guidelines-on-managing-advanced-hiv.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "advanced HIV disease\nIn 2017, WHO published guidelines for managing \nadvanced HIV disease and rapid initiation of \nantiretroviral therapy (5). Advanced HIV disease \nis defined as WHO stage 3 or 4 or a CD4 cell \ncount <200 cells/mm3 for children five years or \nolder. \nAll children younger than five years are \nconsidered to have advanced disease (5). This \nis based on the rationale that most children \nyounger than five years usually present for care \nwith advanced immunosuppression, younger \nchildren have an increased risk of disease \nprogression and mortality regardless of clinical \nand immune",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarizes WHO's 2017 guidance on advanced HIV disease, defining it for children and noting that all children under five are considered to have advanced disease, with exceptions for those stable on treatment. It explains that the evidence base comes mainly from adult trials such as REMSTART and REALITY. It then outlines the screening, prophylaxis and treatment recommendations relevant to children and adolescents, including that cryptococcal screening is not advised under age 10.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
