    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Tuberculosis",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-tuberculosis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Treatment for drug-sensitive TB among children \ncomprises a four-drug regimen that includes \nrifampicin (R), isoniazid (H), pyrazinamide (Z) \nand ethambutol (E). A child-friendly, fixed-\ndose combination, dispersible tablet of RHZ \nis available and can be taken with a 100-mg \nchild friendly formulation of E (intensive-phase \ntreatment). In addition, there is a child-friendly, \ndispersible fixed-dose combination of RH for \nthe continuation phase of treatment. All these \nformulations are available through the Global \nDrug Facility and should be implemented \nto alleviate the pill burden among chi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes child-friendly fixed-dose TB treatment formulations and how to manage drug interactions between rifampicin and lopinavir/ritonavir or dolutegravir in children with HIV.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Severe pneumonia",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-severe-pneumonia.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In studies of severe pneumonia among children \nliving with HIV, the most common bacterial \npathogens isolated were predominantly \nStreptococcus pneumoniae and Staphylococcus \naureus, pathogens that may be effectively \ncovered by commonly available and \nrecommended antibacterial medications (29). The \nseverity of illness and need for hospitalization \nshould be classified according to the WHO \nintegrated management of childhood illness \nalgorithms (30).\nThe use of intravenous or oral co-trimoxazole, \n5 mg of trimethoprim and 25 mg of \nsulfamethoxazole per kg per dose, every six hours \nfor 21 day",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers the bacterial causes of severe pneumonia in children with HIV, classification by IMCI algorithms and high-dose co-trimoxazole for suspected Pneumocystis pneumonia in young infants. It notes that for children aged one to five, standard WHO antibiotics are preferred over co-trimoxazole.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Severe bacterial infections",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-severe-bacterial-infections.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "High rates of bacteraemia have been described \nin children starting antiretroviral therapy, \nespecially within the first three months of \ntreatment (35,36). A recent study of 82 children \nwith malnutrition admitted to a tertiary \nhospital in Durban, South Africa showed that \n6% of admission blood cultures were positive. \nCommunity-associated infections were primarily \nStreptococcus or Staphylococcus. Health \ncare-associated infections were predominantly \ngram-negative (39 of 43, 91%). No pathogen \nwas specifically associated with mortality \n(37). Salmonella is also a common cause of \nbacteraem",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes high rates of bacteraemia in children starting antiretroviral therapy and the pathogens involved, advising treatment according to WHO guidelines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Cryptococcal meningitis",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-cryptococcal-meningitis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Guidelines for cryptococcal meningitis treatment \nhave been revised, and a short-course (one-\nweek) induction regimen with amphotericin B \ndeoxycholate (1.0 mg/kg per day) and flucytosine \n(100 mg/kg per day, divided into four doses per \nday) is the preferred option for children living \nwith HIV. Further recommendations are available \nin the 2018 WHO guidelines (13).",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the preferred one-week amphotericin B and flucytosine induction regimen for cryptococcal meningitis in children with HIV.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Malnutrition",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-malnutrition.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Severe acute malnutrition is common at HIV \ndiagnosis and carries a high risk of mortality. \nThe availability of malnutrition treatment \nprogrammes and commodities, including ready-\nto-use therapeutic food where appropriate, can \nbe considered as essential components of HIV \ntreatment programmes for children.\nUseful resources\nWHO, UNICEF. Statement on the use of child-friendly \nfixed-dose combinations for the treatment of TB \nin children. Geneva: World Health Organization; \n2017 (https://www.who.int/tb/publications/\nChildTbStatementfixed-dose combinations/en)\nPneumonia and bloodstream infectio",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that severe acute malnutrition is common and deadly at HIV diagnosis and that malnutrition treatment should be part of children's HIV programmes. It then lists useful resources on child TB formulations, pneumonia management and malnutrition.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "OPTIMIZE",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-optimize.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In a randomized trial from South Africa, young \nchildren living with HIV (median age 23 months) \nwith severe acute malnutrition were randomized \nto receive antiretroviral therapy within 14 days \nof admission or for antiretroviral therapy to be \ndelayed until nutritional recovery (and after two \nweeks; median time 23 days) (39). The results \nsuggested that a short delay in antiretroviral \ntherapy initiation during early treatment of \nacute malnutrition resulted in improved immune \nrecovery, led to more rapid viral suppression \nand improved anthropometric measures \n(39). In another randomized tr",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews trials from South Africa and Kenya on the timing of antiretroviral therapy in children with severe acute malnutrition or hospitalization. It also stresses the risk of loss to follow-up and the need to link children to ongoing HIV care at discharge.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Counselling and support",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-counselling-and-support.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Additional counselling and support are needed \nwith earlier antiretroviral therapy initiation. \nCounselling tools have been updated to reflect \nnewer optimal regimens and formulations. \nAdolescents generally face challenges in \nadhering to medication, and adolescent-\nfriendly services, including enhanced adherence \ncounselling, adolescent peer counsellors and \nsupport groups, may be especially valuable.\nUseful resources\nUpdate of recommendations on first- and second-line \nantiretroviral regimens: policy brief. Geneva: World \nHealth Organization; 2019 (https://www.who.int/hiv/\npub/arv/arv-updat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Calls for more counselling and adolescent-friendly adherence support with earlier treatment initiation, and lists counselling resources.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
