    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Preventing TB, Pneumocystis pneumonia, bacterial infections and cryptococcal disease",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-preventing-tb-pneumocystis-pneumonia-bacterial-i.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "bacterial infections and cryptococcal \ndisease\nTuberculosis\nTB preventive treatment has been shown to \nreduce morbidity and mortality among children \nliving with HIV one year and older even if they do \nnot have a known TB contact (41). Despite this, \nTB preventive treatment has not yet been fully \nimplemented as part of comprehensive HIV care \nfor children and adolescents (42). TB preventive \ntreatment is currently not recommended for \ninfants living with HIV younger than 12 months \nof age unless they have a known TB contact \n(43). This recommendation is based on evidence \nshowing that TB prev",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers TB preventive treatment for children with HIV, including the restricted use in infants without a TB contact and the preferred six-month isoniazid regimen with pyridoxine. It describes shorter rifampicin- or rifapentine-based options, their interactions with antiretroviral drugs in a summary table, and related resources.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-017",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "PCP and other infections",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-017-pcp-and-other-infections.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Co-trimoxazole\nStudies done in South Africa and Zambia in the \nera before antiretroviral therapy showed a high \nprevalence of Pneumocystis pneumonia, with \ninfants younger than six months being especially \nvulnerable regardless of CD4 cell count (44,45). \nDespite improved access to antiretroviral \ntherapy, Pneumocystis pneumonia remains a \ncommon cause of hospitalization because of \nundiagnosed HIV among infants. Cotrimoxazole \nhas been shown to prevent Pneumocystis \npneumonia, bacterial infections, toxoplasmosis \nand malaria in endemic regions (46). Children \nin Uganda and Zimbabwe were rando",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews evidence on co-trimoxazole prophylaxis to prevent Pneumocystis pneumonia and other infections in children with HIV and when it may be stopped. It also addresses fluconazole and cryptococcal antigen screening for adolescents, including lumbar puncture for those who test positive.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
