    {
      "id": "library/10665-332907/blocks/prose-sec-018",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Vaccinations",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-018-vaccinations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The WHO 2017 advanced HIV disease guidelines \n(5) highlighted the importance of vaccination in \npreventing severe disease among people living \nwith HIV. Several other important vaccines \nwere specifically recommended, including \nmeningococcal, polio, rotavirus and yellow \nfever; however, this publication focuses on \nimmunizations for the vaccine-preventable \ninfections that are most significant to children \nliving with HIV: TB, human papillomavirus (HPV) \nmeasles and pneumococcus.\nBCG\n\t\n■Neonates born to women of unknown HIV \nstatus should be vaccinated, since the benefits \nof BCG vaccination",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out vaccination guidance for children with HIV, focusing on BCG, human papillomavirus, measles and pneumococcal vaccines, including timing relative to antiretroviral therapy and immune recovery. It notes differences in BCG practice between countries and gives revaccination and catch-up advice. A case study describes scaling up the advanced HIV disease package for children in Malawi using a hub-and-spoke model and the challenges encountered.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
