      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Diagnostics\n\t\n■Research gaps related to diagnostics in children include the need to develop simplified point-\nof-care diagnostics for pneumonia, specifically Pneumocystis pneumonia and cytomegalovirus \ndisease.\n\t\n■In addition, the use of rapid molecular tests in stools and other urine LAM assays needs to be \nfurther evaluated in children.\nProphylaxis\n\t\n■Whether there is an optimal package of prophylactic interventions for children living with HIV \nyounger than five years has yet to be determined. This may focus on bacterial infections and \nspecifically on the benefit of azithromycin.\n\t\n■TB pre",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists clinical research gaps in diagnostics, prophylaxis and treatment for children with advanced HIV disease, such as point-of-care pneumonia tests, the role of azithromycin and shorter TB preventive regimens. It also mentions ongoing studies of cryptococcal meningitis treatment that may inform care of older children.",
